-- Passage rg9-comparing-texts-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('cce15e04-4a2f-5fc0-9fc3-dd9a766dd461', 'rg9-comparing-texts-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-comparing-texts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Proud Start$q$, $q$Text 1: The Pine Who Grew Too Fast (an invented fable)

On a windy ridge, a young pine shot upward faster than any tree around it. "Look at me," it called to the old oaks below. "In one summer I have passed you all." The oaks said nothing. They spent that summer pushing their roots deep into the rocky soil, slowly and without any show.

When autumn gales swept across the ridge, the tall pine swayed and creaked. Its thin roots held only the loose top layer of earth, and by the first snow it leaned so far that its crown nearly brushed the ground. The oaks stood straight. In spring, the bent pine finally understood that height is easy to see, but what holds a tree up is hidden below.

Text 2: First Race (a poem that retells the fable)

The starter's whistle, and I'm gone,
the front of the pack in the first long field,
my teammates fading into a blur.
I think: this course will have to yield.

The seniors run behind me, calm,
their breathing even, their faces plain.
They've logged their miles in August heat
and early mornings in the rain.

The second hill is where it starts.
My legs turn heavy, then turn to stone.
One by one they glide on past,
and I am climbing the hill alone.

I finish twenty-third today.
The coach just nods and shows me the chart:
the miles beneath the seniors' names,
the roots that never showed at the start.$q$, 'draft')
