-- Passage rg9-comparing-texts-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('2c170014-94cb-5ba5-958f-05a46a079359', 'rg9-comparing-texts-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-comparing-texts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Carrying Words$q$, $q$Text 1: The Mountain Messenger (an invented legend)

High in a range of invented mountains lived Peleku, the runner who carried news between seven villages. Each village spoke its own language, and Peleku knew them all. When a shepherd in the eastern valley needed to warn of an early frost, Peleku ran the message west. When a weaver in the west wished to trade cloth for honey, Peleku carried the offer east. People trusted him because he never added a word or dropped one.

Yet Peleku spent so many days on the trails that he rarely slept in his own village. The children there knew his name but not his face. On the night he finally retired, the seven villages sent gifts, and he sat by his own fire for the first time in years, wondering whether he belonged to every village or to none.

Text 2: Appointment at Two (a story that retells the legend)

Every other Tuesday, Ana Lucia Ferreira left school early to go with her grandmother to the clinic. Her grandmother spoke Portuguese; the nurse spoke English. Ana sat between them and turned each sentence into the other language, careful not to soften the nurse's instructions or skip her grandmother's questions.

At first she felt proud. Lately she felt tired. She had missed two chemistry labs, and her friends had stopped saving her a seat at lunch on Tuesdays. On the bus home, she wondered whether she was part of her grandmother's world, part of her friends' world, or stuck in the space between.

That evening her grandmother placed a plate of warm bread in front of her and said, in slow English, "Thank you, my bridge." Ana laughed, then answered in Portuguese. For the first time, the space between felt less like a gap and more like a place she could stand.$q$, 'draft')
