-- Passage rg9-connections-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('0b3e2f97-e0df-50a8-9561-14ff40e5b1d3', 'rg9-connections-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-connections'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Line That Changed the Game$q$, $q$For its first forty years, the Delmore Valley Basketball League had no three-point line. Every basket from the floor counted for two points, no matter how far away the shooter stood. Because a long shot was harder to make and earned no extra reward, coaches told their players to work the ball close to the basket. The tallest players were the stars, and most games were slow and crowded near the hoop.

In 1988, the league's rules committee voted to paint an arc on every court, about six and a half meters from the basket. Any shot made from behind the arc would now count for three points. The committee hoped the change would make games more exciting for fans, whose numbers had been falling for several seasons.

The first year brought little change. Most coaches still trusted the old style, and teams attempted only a few long shots per game. The turning point came in 1991, when a small team from Pell Harbor, which had no tall players at all, won the championship by taking more three-point shots than any team in league history.

Other coaches studied Pell Harbor's games and began copying the approach. Within a decade, the average team was attempting four times as many three-point shots as before. This change reshaped the kinds of players teams looked for. Quick, accurate shooters became as valuable as tall players, and players spread out across the whole court instead of crowding near the basket.

The committee got its wish. Games became faster and higher scoring, and attendance rose for twelve straight seasons. A single painted line had changed not only how points were scored, but who got to play.$q$, 'draft')
