values ('4f90803e-61d5-522f-b155-61b684912c27', 'rg9-connections-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-connections'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Glaze Nobody Planned$q$, $q$The village of Tervo Ridge has made pottery for more than two hundred years. For most of that time, its potters were known for plain brown jugs and bowls, coated with a glaze made from local clay and wood ash. The pots were sturdy and useful, but few people outside the region had heard of them.

In the winter of 1957, a potter named Anneli Mwangi-Haro ran short of the usual hardwood for her kiln. She burned a load of pine branches instead, along with some old fence posts from her family's farm. When she opened the kiln two days later, she expected the usual brown. Instead, many of the pots were streaked with a deep, glassy green.

Mwangi-Haro was puzzled, and she spent the next year trying to repeat the result. She eventually learned that the fence posts had been coated long ago with a copper-based paint. During the firing, ash from the burning posts had floated onto the pots and melted into the glaze. The copper in the ash was what turned it green.

Once she understood the cause, she no longer needed old fence posts. She began adding small, measured amounts of copper to her glaze and firing her kiln the same way every time. The green became reliable rather than accidental.

Other potters in Tervo Ridge soon learned the method from her. Within ten years, the village's green pottery was being sold in cities hundreds of miles away, and young people who might have moved away for work stayed to learn the craft. A shortage of firewood, followed by a year of patient testing, had given the village a new identity.$q$, 'draft')
