-- Passage rg9-context-clues-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('43f3d9bc-e288-5761-ae02-1de7bbc300fb', 'rg9-context-clues-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-context-clues'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Balloons That Read the Sky$q$, $q$Twice a day, at hundreds of weather stations around the world, someone walks outside and lets go of a balloon. The balloon carries a small box of instruments, about the size of a juice carton, that measures temperature, humidity and air pressure as it rises. Forecasters depend on these measurements to predict what the sky will do over the next few days.

The balloon is filled with a gas that is lighter than air, which makes it buoyant: like a beach ball held under water and then released, it is pushed upward by the heavier air around it. As it begins to ascend, it climbs about five meters every second, rising far above the height where passenger jets fly.

The higher the balloon goes, the thinner the air becomes, and the balloon swells to many times its starting size. At last the rubber stretches too far and bursts. A small parachute opens, and the instrument box drifts back to the ground. Because the box weighs less than a pair of sneakers and falls slowly, the danger it poses to people below is negligible, so small that it is hardly worth worrying about.

During the flight, the box sends its readings by radio to the station below. Those numbers are not kept in one place. They are dispersed to forecasting centers in many countries, spread out across a worldwide network within minutes of being recorded.

Satellites now gather huge amounts of weather data from space, and some people wonder whether the balloons are still needed. Most forecasters say yes. Satellites view the atmosphere from far above, but a balloon passes directly through each layer and measures it from the inside. When forecasters tried running their computer models without balloon data, their predictions grew noticeably worse, which is why many of them call the balloons indispensable.$q$, 'draft')
