-- Passage rg9-context-clues-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('5439b18a-a4f1-5547-9e26-ad34e22bc669', 'rg9-context-clues-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-context-clues'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Threads on the Loom$q$, $q$The loom took up half of Aunt Adaeze's sunroom, a wooden frame taller than Nkechi and crossed by hundreds of threads. Nkechi had never woven anything before, and she felt like a complete novice, a beginner who did not even know the names of the parts.

Her aunt started with the basics. "These long threads that run from the top of the loom to the bottom are the warp," she said. "They stay in place the whole time. Everything else is woven across them." Nkechi touched one, half expecting it to bend, but it was stretched as straight as a guitar string.

"Why do they have to be so tight?" Nkechi asked.

"If the warp threads are loose, the cloth comes out lumpy and uneven," Aunt Adaeze said. "They have to stay taut, pulled firmly in place, or nothing lines up."

For the first week, Nkechi wove plain stripes, passing a wooden shuttle back and forth. She studied her aunt's finished blankets, which hung on the wall. Their patterns were intricate: tiny diamonds nested inside larger diamonds, and borders made of dozens of small repeating shapes in five colors. Nkechi could not even tell where one shape ended and the next began.

On Saturday she made a mistake near the bottom of her scarf and tried to fix it by tugging one thread. Instead, a whole row came loose, and then another. "Stop pulling," her aunt said gently, "or the entire piece will unravel." Together they carefully wove the loose threads back into place.

By the end of the month, Nkechi's scarf was finished, uneven at one edge but warm and bright. Her aunt hung it on the wall beside the blankets.$q$, 'draft')
