values ('b3d29201-729c-5bf3-ad92-e68fe982d3cb', 'rg9-figurative-language-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-figurative-language'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Match Point$q$, $q$The gym at Riverside High was packed for the volleyball semifinal, and the bleachers shook every time the home crowd stamped its feet. Amara Okafor stood at the service line, spinning the ball in her hands while the noise rolled over her like a wave.

Her team had lost the first set badly. In the huddle afterward, Coach Delgado had called it "a learning experience," and nobody had to ask what he really meant. They had served into the net four times and let easy balls drop between them.

The second set was different. Amara's sister, Ngozi, played libero, and she dove for everything. She was not reckless, Amara thought, just fearless, and there was a difference. Ngozi always knew exactly where the floor was before she threw herself at it.

Now the score was tied in the deciding set, and the next point could end the match. Across the net, the other team's tallest hitter, a girl named Sofia Brandt, was bouncing on her toes. Sofia had been confident all night. Some of her teammates had been something closer to cocky, pointing at the scoreboard after every kill.

Amara tossed the ball and served. It floated, wobbled, and dropped just inside the back line. For a moment the gym held its breath. Then the line judge's flag went down, and the bleachers exploded.

Later, in the parking lot, Ngozi described the serve as "awfully good." Amara laughed. Coming from her sister, there was no higher praise.$q$, 'draft')
