   $q$The passage compares "castoffs" and "hand-me-downs." What is the main difference between these two words?$q$, $q$[{"text":"\"Castoffs\" are clothes, while \"hand-me-downs\" are furniture.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"\"Castoffs\" sounds like worthless trash, while \"hand-me-downs\" sounds warm and familiar.","is_correct":true},{"text":"\"Castoffs\" is a euphemism, while \"hand-me-downs\" is a harsh insult.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reverses-meaning"},{"text":"\"Castoffs\" are given to family, while \"hand-me-downs\" are sold to strangers.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reverses-meaning"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$"Castoffs" sounds like worthless trash, while "hand-me-downs" sounds warm and familiar.$q$, $q$The author says "castoffs" suggests something "thrown away because it had no value," while "hand-me-downs" is "warmer, connected to families and siblings," though both describe "the same kind of object." The connotations run the other way from the insult choice.$q$, 'draft', 'c715a5ec-fabf-5e3c-b191-c6859aba3332')
