-- Passage rg9-figurative-language-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('b529d6f7-7092-5e3d-9d11-a9c17f8c195f', 'rg9-figurative-language-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-figurative-language'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Scenic Route$q$, $q$Our family car was older than I was, and Dad called it "a classic." Mom called it "the dinosaur." The air conditioner had quit two summers earlier, so on the drive to Aunt Rosalind's lake house, all four windows were down and my little brother, Teo, kept sticking his hand out to surf the wind.

About two hours in, Dad missed the exit. Instead of turning around, he announced that we were taking "the scenic route." Mom raised one eyebrow but said nothing. The scenic route turned out to be a narrow road that wound through cornfields so tall and so identical that I started to feel like we were driving in circles.

Then the engine began to make a sound like a spoon rattling in an empty pot. Dad pulled onto the gravel shoulder and opened the hood. Steam drifted out in a slow gray ribbon.

"It's a minor crisis," Dad said, which was the kind of phrase that only made sense in our family.

A farmer named Mr. Abernathy stopped his truck beside us. He did not lecture or tease. He simply handed Dad a jug of water and waited while the engine cooled. When Teo asked if the car was going to die, Mr. Abernathy laughed and said it was "just catching its breath."

We reached the lake house three hours late. Aunt Rosalind met us on the porch, took one look at our sunburned arms, and said, "I see you came the long way." Dad grinned. Mom finally laughed too. That night, sitting by the water, I decided the scenic route had been worth it, though I would never say so out loud.$q$, 'draft')
