-- Passage rg9-figurative-language-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('21297116-8a38-59cf-b06a-e1d983fd9485', 'rg9-figurative-language-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-figurative-language'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Bicycle Craze$q$, $q$In the 1890s, a strange fever swept through many cities in the United States and Europe. It was not an illness. It was a passion for bicycles, and newspapers of the time called it a craze.

Earlier bicycles had been difficult machines. One design, with a giant front wheel and a tiny rear wheel, placed the rider high in the air, and a sudden stop could send him flying over the handlebars. Another early model had iron tires on wooden wheels. Riders nicknamed it the "boneshaker," and the name was not meant as a compliment.

The change came with the so-called safety bicycle. Its two wheels were the same size, its pedals turned a chain, and soon its tires were filled with air. For the first time, an ordinary person could ride comfortably on a city street. Factories could hardly keep up with demand.

The bicycle brought a quiet revolution. There were no marches or speeches, but daily life changed. People who had never owned a horse could now travel several miles to work or to visit friends. Clubs organized weekend rides into the countryside. Many young women found that the bicycle gave them a new kind of independence, and some even changed the way they dressed so they could ride more easily.

Not everyone was pleased. Some critics called cyclists a nuisance, complaining that they frightened horses and raced through crowded streets. Within a decade, however, the craze had cooled into something steadier. The bicycle was no longer a fad. It had become an everyday tool, and in many places, it still is.$q$, 'draft')
