  ('0598f471-d2c2-5473-9302-2aac540e9cf4', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-figurative-language'), null, 'hard',
   $q$The passage uses "craze" and later "fad" for the bicycle's popularity. What do these words suggest that "everyday tool" does not?$q$, $q$[{"text":"That the bicycle had become permanent and was here to stay.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reverses-meaning"},{"text":"That the excitement was wild and might fade as quickly as it came.","is_correct":true},{"text":"That the bicycle was expensive and only wealthy people could buy one.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"That the bicycle was useful for getting to work and visiting friends.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-target"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$That the excitement was wild and might fade as quickly as it came.$q$, $q$"Craze" and "fad" suggest sudden, intense, short-lived popularity, which is why the author says the craze "cooled" and the bicycle was "no longer a fad." "Everyday tool" is the term for something lasting, not the opposite.$q$, 'draft', '21297116-8a38-59cf-b06a-e1d983fd9485')
