   $q$Ms. Petrakis tells new workers to say "Let me find out" instead of "I don't know." Why might she prefer the first phrase?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Because it allows the worker to avoid helping the customer.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reverses-meaning"},{"text":"Because it is shorter and faster to say to a busy customer.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"Because it means the worker already knows the answer.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-denotation"},{"text":"Because it admits not knowing while promising helpful action.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Because it admits not knowing while promising helpful action.$q$, $q$Both phrases admit the worker lacks the answer, but "Let me find out" adds a promise to help, giving it a positive connotation. It does not mean the worker already knows, since it still admits a gap in knowledge.$q$, 'draft', 'd9955455-305e-502d-934b-7b6201903b64')
