   $q$Jun imagines "Fine!" as smiling and "sure" as shrugging. What does this show about words with similar meanings?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Short replies should never be used between close friends.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"Words with similar meanings always create exactly the same feeling.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignores-connotation"},{"text":"Only long messages can show how a person really feels.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"overgeneralizes"},{"text":"Small differences in wording or punctuation can change the feeling a reply gives.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Small differences in wording or punctuation can change the feeling a reply gives.$q$, $q$"Fine!", "sure," and "Fine." all mean yes, but Jun pictures a different attitude for each one. That shows that words with the same basic meaning can carry different feelings, the opposite of the first choice.$q$, 'draft', 'c639ac32-73f1-5922-9044-9b583b0e2340')
