   $q$How does the structure of the fable prepare the reader for its moral?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It focuses only on the farmer's choices, so the moral is about farming.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-narrow"},{"text":"It builds up Ash's false belief, tests it with her absence, and ends on the pigeon's friendship, which matches both halves of the moral.","is_correct":true},{"text":"It tells events out of order so the reader cannot tell what caused what.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-sequence"},{"text":"It begins with the moral and then gives an example that proves it false.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-structure"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It builds up Ash's false belief, tests it with her absence, and ends on the pigeon's friendship, which matches both halves of the moral.$q$, $q$The rooster turning without Ash supports "the world may turn without our help," and the pigeon missing her supports the second half. The moral comes at the end, not the beginning.$q$, 'draft', 'bc03d963-336b-5c89-9d5d-7385b0af5a55')
