   $q$How does the first-person notebook point of view shape what the reader learns about the narrator's feelings?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The narrator mostly records facts, so feelings come through small details, such as shaking hands, that the reader must interpret.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The narrator hides all feelings, so the reader cannot tell how the narrator feels about the count.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-extreme"},{"text":"The narrator explains every feeling directly, so nothing is left for the reader to figure out.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reverses-meaning"},{"text":"The notebook is written by Grandpa, so the reader learns only his feelings.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-narrator"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The narrator mostly records facts, so feelings come through small details, such as shaking hands, that the reader must interpret.$q$, $q$Entries are brief and factual, yet "My hands were shaking" and saving the notebook for Grandpa reveal excitement and love indirectly. The reader can tell how the narrator feels, just not from direct statements.$q$, 'draft', '2b0c2234-33df-5024-85a0-9bf1538354e1')
