-- Passage rg9-literary-structure-pov-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('23ce4e8e-1ef2-5e5a-9ca0-c6c80479b761', 'rg9-literary-structure-pov-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-literary-structure-pov'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Two Names$q$, $q$At the airport in Accra, your grandmother calls you Kwabena, and for a second you look behind you for whoever she means.

At home in Ohio you are Ben. Your teachers call you Ben, your soccer coach calls you Ben, and your best friend calls you B, which is even shorter. Kwabena is the name on your birth certificate and on the cake your mother orders every year, but you have never heard anyone shout it across a crowded room.

Your grandmother's house has a mango tree in the yard and a radio that is always on. Your cousins speak Twi to each other and English to you, switching so smoothly that you do not notice until the second day. On the third day, your cousin Efua teaches you how to bargain for oranges at the market. You get it wrong. The woman at the stall laughs, but not unkindly, and sells you six for the price of four anyway, because, Efua explains, you tried.

By the end of the second week, when your grandmother calls "Kwabena" from the porch, you are already turning around before she finishes the name.

Years from now, you will introduce yourself at a college orientation, and the student leading the circle will ask what you like to be called. You will pause, the way you paused at the airport, and you will be surprised by which name arrives first.

But that is later. Right now, you are on the porch with a bag of oranges you paid too much for, and your grandmother is laughing at the story, and the radio is playing a song everyone else knows.$q$, 'draft')
