-- Passage rg9-main-idea-summary-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('b54f0d06-1f55-5dce-931a-66ac5b5db4b1', 'rg9-main-idea-summary-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Threads Beneath the Forest$q$, $q$When people look at a forest, they usually notice trunks, branches and leaves. But some of the most important activity happens out of sight. In the soil, many trees are linked to fungi, living things that grow as networks of thin threads much finer than a human hair.

The partnership benefits both sides. The fungal threads wrap around or grow into the tips of tree roots. Because the threads spread far through the soil, they can collect water and minerals, such as phosphorus, that the roots would struggle to reach on their own. In return, the tree passes along some of the sugar it makes from sunlight. The fungi cannot make their own food, so this trade keeps them alive.

In a study in the fictional Larchmont Valley, forest scientist Amara Kovacs and her students tested how much young trees depended on these partners. They planted seedlings in two kinds of soil: one with the usual fungi and one in which the fungi had been removed. After two growing seasons, the seedlings in soil with fungi were, on average, almost twice as tall. They also survived a dry summer far better.

Kovacs is careful not to overstate the findings. Some reports claim that trees use the fungal threads to send warnings or share food with their neighbors on purpose. She notes that scientists are still debating how much material actually moves between trees through these threads, and why. What is clear, she says, is that many trees grow poorly without their fungal partners.

That lesson matters for people who replant forests after fires or logging. Crews in Larchmont Valley now test the soil before planting. Where fungi are missing, they add a small amount of healthy forest soil to each hole, giving the seedlings a partner from the start.$q$, 'draft')
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body
