-- Passage rg9-main-idea-summary-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('f8986b94-c83e-5075-9917-4d2b7cdc8c2d', 'rg9-main-idea-summary-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Running the Second Half Faster$q$, $q$Most new cross-country runners make the same mistake. When the starting signal sounds, excitement takes over, and they sprint the first part of the race as if it were the whole race. By the final mile, their legs feel heavy, and runners who started more slowly pass them one by one.

Coach Delphine Achterberg of the fictional Riverbend High School team wants her athletes to do the opposite. She teaches a plan called a negative split, which means running the second half of a race faster than the first. The idea sounds backward, but it rests on how the body uses energy. A runner who starts too fast builds up fatigue early and must slow down to recover. A runner who starts at a steady, controlled pace saves energy for a strong finish.

Learning this plan takes practice. Achterberg has her runners wear simple watches during training and check their time at each half-mile marker. At first, many of them find the opening pace frustratingly slow. Over several weeks, they learn what an even effort feels like without looking at the watch. She also teaches them to pick out runners ahead and pass them steadily in the last part of the race.

The results have encouraged the team. During one season, Achterberg kept records of each runner's times. Runners who followed the plan improved their finishing times by an average of forty seconds. Just as important, she says, they enjoyed the races more because they felt strong at the end instead of exhausted.

The plan does not work perfectly in every race. Hilly courses and very hot days can upset even careful pacing. Still, Achterberg believes that learning patience at the start is one of the most valuable skills a young runner can gain.$q$, 'draft')
