-- Passage rg9-main-idea-summary-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('039e26ac-b513-5abf-8a33-47a590660b5c', 'rg9-main-idea-summary-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Return of the Hand-Painted Sign$q$, $q$A century ago, almost every shop sign in the fictional city of Port Alder was painted by hand. Sign painters climbed ladders with brushes and cans of enamel, lettering the names of bakeries, barbershops and hardware stores in bold, careful strokes. Their work gave each street its own look.

By the 1980s, the trade had nearly disappeared. Machines could cut letters from vinyl sheets and print large signs quickly and cheaply. Shop owners chose the faster option, and most sign painters retired without training anyone to replace them.

In recent years, however, hand-painted signs have begun to return to Port Alder. Part of the reason is that some business owners want their shops to stand out. A sign painted by a skilled artist has small differences in each letter that a printed sign lacks. Lucia Fernhill, who owns a corner bakery, says customers often stop to photograph her painted window and then come inside.

The revival also depends on new painters learning old skills. Ingrid Matsuda, one of the last painters from the earlier era, began teaching evening classes at a community art center. Her students practice the same basic strokes for weeks before they paint a single word. They learn to mix paint so that it flows smoothly and to plan the spacing of letters so a sign can be read from across the street.

The trade remains small. A painted sign takes longer to make and costs more than a printed one, and not every shop can afford it. Yet Matsuda's students have now painted more than sixty signs around the city. For many residents, these signs are a reminder that a street can show the care of the people who work on it.$q$, 'draft')
