-- Passage rg9-story-details-inference-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('aabf52a7-4a3e-5097-af50-5e286c452e39', 'rg9-story-details-inference-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-story-details-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$No Rounding Up$q$, $q$For the summer meteor count, Ms. Takahashi had given Jonah three rules: no phone screens, no flashlights except the red one, and no rounding up. "If you are not sure you saw it," she said, "it did not happen."

They lay on folding chairs in the field behind the water tower, a clipboard balanced on Ms. Takahashi's knee. She had been sending meteor counts to the state university for twenty-two summers. Jonah had agreed to come because his mother said it would be good for him to get out of the house, and because Ms. Takahashi had promised hot chocolate.

The first hour was slow. Jonah saw four streaks and called them out. Ms. Takahashi wrote down three. When he asked about the fourth, she pointed to a steady blinking light still crawling across the sky and said it was a plane. He felt his face get warm and was glad it was dark.

By the second hour his eyes had adjusted. He began to notice faint meteors he would have missed before, thin scratches of light that vanished almost before they began. He stopped calling out the ones he was unsure about. Twice Ms. Takahashi asked, "Did you get that one?" and twice he answered, "I think so, but I'm not sure." She nodded and did not write it down.

At two in the morning she handed him the clipboard and the red flashlight. "Your count," she said. "I'm going to close my eyes for a minute."

Jonah counted for forty minutes alone. He recorded nine meteors and left off two he was not certain about. When he finished, Ms. Takahashi was awake and watching him, her hot chocolate untouched and cold in its thermos.

On the way home she said the university would list both their names on the report this year. Jonah said that seemed like a lot of credit for forty minutes. She said the minutes were not the part that mattered.$q$, 'draft')
