-- Passage rg9-story-details-inference-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('35c073ad-d385-59ff-94db-6d992a259a4d', 'rg9-story-details-inference-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-story-details-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Cue Forty-One$q$, $q$The spring musical had sixty-three lighting cues, and Bao had memorized all of them by the second week of rehearsal. He was not supposed to run the lighting board. That was Keisha Abernathy's job, and she had run it for three years. Bao's job was to sit beside her in the booth, follow the script with a pencil, and say "standby" ten seconds before each cue so she could get ready.

On opening night, during the first act, Keisha leaned over and whispered that she felt dizzy. She had skipped dinner to finish painting the set. Bao handed her his granola bar. She ate it in two bites and kept going, but her hands were shaking on the sliders, and she missed cue twenty-two by almost a full second. On stage, the actress playing the mayor delivered her big line in the dark, and somebody in the third row laughed.

At intermission Ms. Ferreira, the director, came up to the booth. She looked at Keisha, then at Bao. "Can you run act two?" she asked him.

Bao said yes before he had time to be scared. Keisha moved to his chair and took the script. For the whole second act she called standby for him in a voice so calm that he almost forgot two hundred people were watching. He hit every cue. Cue forty-one, the slow sunrise during the final song, took eleven seconds to bring up, and he counted each one under his breath.

When the curtain came down, the cast went out for their bows. Ms. Ferreira's voice came over the headset: "Booth, bring up the house lights." Bao reached for the slider, then stopped. He slid the board a few inches to the left, in front of Keisha.

She looked at him. Then she pushed the slider up, and the lights over the audience slowly came on.$q$, 'draft')
