-- Passage rg9-story-details-inference-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('f4c42000-e62e-5d28-9ec4-66a595b9f8e0', 'rg9-story-details-inference-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-story-details-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Green Button$q$, $q$Every Sunday afternoon, Nadia Farouk rode her bike across town to teach her grandfather how to use the tablet her aunt had mailed him from Lisbon. The plan was simple. Once Grandpa Samir could make a video call by himself, he could see Aunt Rania and the cousins whenever he liked, and Nadia could have her Sundays back for soccer.

The first week she showed him the green button. He nodded and wrote GREEN BUTTON on a notepad in careful capital letters. The second week he could not find the notepad. The third week he found the notepad but said the button had moved. Nadia explained that buttons on a screen did not move. He said these ones seemed to.

Each visit ran longer than the last. After the lesson he would make tea, and then he would ask about her classes, and then he would show her a photo album she had already seen and tell her the stories anyway. The stories changed a little each time, but the ending was always the same: her grandmother laughing at something he had said.

On the fifth Sunday Nadia arrived early. Through the kitchen window she saw Grandpa Samir at the table with the tablet propped against the sugar bowl. He was on a video call. She could hear Aunt Rania's voice, and she watched him tap the screen to switch cameras so he could show off the tomato plants on the windowsill. He did it without looking at his notepad. When the call ended, he slid the tablet under a stack of newspapers.

Nadia stood on the step for a minute. Then she knocked, and when he opened the door, she said she thought they should start again with the green button, because it was a tricky one. He agreed that it was. She did not mention soccer at all, and the following week she brought her own photo album.$q$, 'draft')
