-- Passage rg9-text-structure-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('83742d3c-013e-5dc6-b3f3-268062f7c544', 'rg9-text-structure-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-text-structure'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Why Bread Goes Stale$q$, $q$Most people believe that bread goes stale because it dries out. Leave a loaf on the counter for a few days, the thinking goes, and the moisture escapes into the air, leaving the bread hard and crumbly. It is a sensible guess, but food scientists say it is mostly wrong.

The real cause lies in starch, the main ingredient in flour. When dough bakes, heat and water cause the tightly packed starch grains to swell and loosen, much like a coiled spring relaxing. This loose arrangement is what makes fresh bread soft. As the bread cools and sits, however, the starch slowly packs itself back into tight, orderly bundles. The bread becomes firm even if it loses almost no water at all.

A simple experiment shows the difference. Researchers at the Kestrel Valley Food Lab sealed slices of bread in airtight bags so that no moisture could escape. After three days, the sealed slices had become just as stale as slices left in the open. Moisture loss alone could not explain the change.

The same idea explains a puzzle many cooks have noticed. Bread kept in the refrigerator goes stale faster than bread kept at room temperature, because cool temperatures speed up the repacking of starch. Freezing, on the other hand, stops the process almost completely.

This knowledge leads to practical advice. To keep bread fresh for a day or two, store it in a sealed bag on the counter. For longer storage, freeze it. And when a loaf has already turned firm, a few minutes in a hot oven will loosen the starch again and bring back much of its softness, at least until it cools.$q$, 'draft')
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body
where public.passages.status = 'draft';
