insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('113f0458-6fc3-5ad7-bddb-576dc8559945', 'rg9-theme-summary-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-theme-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Box 14$q$, $q$Amara Bello had signed up to volunteer at the county historical society because she pictured herself holding a general's orders or a governor's speech. Instead, Mr. Halvorsen, the society's only full-time employee, set a gray cardboard box on the table in front of her. The label said BOX 14: CLAYTON SCHOOL LETTERS, 1901 TO 1909.

"A teacher's letters," he said. "Nobody has read them in a century. Type each one up exactly, misspellings included."

The first letter asked the school board for more chalk. The second asked for more chalk. The third complained, politely, that the stove smoked whenever the wind came from the west. Amara typed slowly and checked the clock often.

The teacher's name was Ida Marsh, and by the second week Amara could recognize her handwriting from across the table. Ida wrote about a boy who could not see the board until someone found him a pair of spectacles. She wrote about saving part of her salary for three years to buy the school a globe, because the children had never seen the shape of the whole world at once. She wrote about the stove again.

Near the bottom of the box was a letter in different handwriting, dated 1931. A man named Walter Pike was writing to the school board to ask whether Miss Marsh was still living. He was a surveyor now, he wrote, and he had measured land in four states. He had first understood what a map meant while spinning her globe.

Amara read that one twice before she typed it.

When she finished the box, Mr. Halvorsen asked whether she wanted something more exciting next. She looked at the shelf, where Boxes 15 through 40 waited in a long gray row.

"What's in Box 15?" she asked.$q$, 'draft')
